In the above solution, we use `ReentrantLock` for locking the critical section of code where the synchronization is needed. This ensures that only one thread can execute the critical section at any given time. The `lock.read()` method returns the current value of the variable and then increments by 1, and finally writes back the incremented value. This way, the critical section is executed in order, preventing race conditions. If the ticket is zero, an exception is thrown. Otherwise, the counter is updated based on the ticket number. Note: In real-world applications, you should not rely on static variables like `Float.MAX_VALUE`, but instead use dynamic values or constants. Also, make sure to handle exceptions properly when necessary.

